#  SketchUp model builder (works in SketchUp 2017 ... 2025, Pro or Make)
# =============================================================================
#  HOW TO USE
#   1. Keep this .rb file and the "textures" folder together in one folder.
#   2. Open SketchUp with a new, empty model (template: Architectural - Meters).
#   3. Window > Ruby Console   (SketchUp 2021+: Extensions > Developer > Ruby Console)
#   4. Type   load "C:/path/to/Villa_G153_Elevation.rb"   and press Enter.
#      (Mac example:  load "/Users/you/Desktop/Villa_G153/Villa_G153_Elevation.rb")
#   5. Wait 10-40 s.  Then File > Save As...  ->  Villa_G153_Elevation.skp
#
#  WHAT YOU GET
#   * ONE component "Villa G153 - Typical House" (house + plot + front wall +
#     garden + sidewalk strip).  Edit it once -> every house in the gated
#     street updates, because all plots are instances of the same component.
#   * HOUSES_IN_STREET identical instances side by side (12.50 m plots) and a
#     street (road, curbs, opposite sidewalk) - set the constant below to 1
#     for a single house.
#   * Tags (layers) for every system: massing, travertine, bronze, glazing,
#     timber, balcony, roof pavilion, boundary wall, landscape, lighting,
#     curtains, site.  Every element is a named group (see Outliner).
#   * Scenes: 01 Front Elevation (parallel projection), 02-05 perspectives,
#     shadows set for Basra (30.5 N, 47.8 E) on an October afternoon.
#
#  PLAN FIDELITY
#   All wall lines are taken from the client DWG (GF + FF + roof plans,
#   plot 12.50 x 20.00 m).  Exterior walls / plan form are unchanged; added
#   only facade layers, frame, balcony details, pergola, canopies, screens,
#   boundary wall, landscape.  Levels (not given in the DWG - adjust if your
#   section differs):
#       ground / sidewalk     +0.00        GF finished floor   +0.45
#       FF finished floor     +3.95        roof finished level +7.45
#       parapet / frame top   +8.45        roof pavilion top    +10.20
#   Axes: red X = along the street (0 = left plot line seen from the street),
#         green Y = into the plot (0 = front boundary), blue Z = up.
# =============================================================================
